:"2023"}
{"abstract":"The Sillai Patti Carbonatite Complex constitutes the second largest carbonatite complex in Pakistan. However, our current understanding of its origin and geodynamic settings remains unclear. An integrated investigation, involving mineral composition, titanite U[sbnd]Pb dating, whole-rock geochemistry and C[sbnd]O isotopes as well as in situ Sr[sbnd]Nd isotope study of the mineral calcite and titanite from this carbonatite complex was conducted. The carbonatite, mainly consisting of calcite, apatite, amphibole and biotite, occurs as a sheet-like intrusion along an NNE-SSW-striking and southward dipping thrust plane. Titanite U[sbnd]Pb dating results reveal that the Sillai Patti carbonatites were formed at about 83 ± 7 Ma. Chemically, the Sillai Patti carbonatites are characterized by variably high SiO2 (4.56–24.02 wt%), P2O5 (3.32–6.09 wt%) and total REEs contents (1989–3309 ppm) and are enriched in alkalis (Na2O > K2O), Nb (29–397 ppm), Zr, Ba, Sr and LREEs. The elevated contents of REEs and Nb and the ubiquitous REE-Nb mineral hosts (e.g., fluorapatite (up to 1.8 wt% LREE2O3), titanite (up to 7360 ppm REE and 10,050 ppm Nb), calcite (350–760 ppm REE), allanite, pyrochlore, zircon and monazite) in the Sillai Patti carbonatites suggest that they have considerable potential of REEs and Nb mineralization. δ13CPDB and δ18OSMOW values for the Sillai Patti carbonatites vary from −6.7 ‰ to −6.9 ‰ and 8.3 ‰ to 10.4 ‰, respectively, which are in good agreement with primary mantle-derived carbonatite values. The Sillai Patti carbonatites show slightly enriched Sr and Nd isotopic compositions ((87Sr/86Sr)i = 0.70472–0.70496; εNd(t) = −2.6 to −0.1), indicating derivation from an enriched sub-continental lithospheric mantle source. We propose that the Sillai Patti carbonatites were formed in an intra-continental rift setting. Along with other analogous Late Cretaceous carbonatites and alkaline silicate rocks in the northwestern margin of the Indian plate, they likely recorded an early period of alkaline magmatism related to the Réunion hotspot mantle plume.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Zhu, Yu Xiang","Wang, Lian Xun","Khattak, Nimat Ullah","Ma, Chang Qian","Luo, Gen Ming","Ulrich, Thomas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-133417"],"filters":{"added":"2023-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Asia, South"],"corresponding_author":["Wang, Lian Xun"],"country":["China","Pakistan"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","China University of Geosciences, Wuhan","University of Peshawar"],"keyword":["Carbonatite","Mantle plume","Pakistan"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"petrogenesis of the late cretaceous sillai patti carbonatites nw pakistan insights from c o sr nd isotopes and titanite u sbnd pb dating","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85148941289","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-133417","identifier":[{"id":"85148941289","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-07273216-f588-49b0-bc52-4de53491009a"],"keyword":["C-O-Sr-Nd isotopes","Carbonatite","Mantle plume","Pakistan","Sillai Patti","Titanite U[sbnd]Pb dating"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-66E3C6A3D2B342B7A6C1A57E4F017662"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"china university of geosciences wuhan","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"China University of Geosciences, Wuhan","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"pk","key":"university of peshawar","loc":["Pakistan","Asia, South"],"name":"University of Peshawar","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Geoscience"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Yu Xiang","key":"zhupau","last":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yu Xiang","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lian Xun","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lian Xun","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nimat Ullah","key":"khattakpau","last":"Khattak","name":"Khattak, Nimat Ullah","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Chang Qian","key":"mapau","last":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chang Qian","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gen Ming","key":"luopau","last":"Luo","name":"Luo, Gen Ming","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"ulrichpau","last":"Ulrich","locid":"2f17303e-f53d-44ba-8f79-19298ed879fe","name":"Ulrich, Thomas","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.lithos.2023.107087","issn":["00244937","18726143"],"issn_org":["00244937"],"journal":"Lithos","paperid":"107087","publisher":"Elsevier BV","publisher_org":"Elsevier B.V.","status":"p","title":"Lithos","volume":"442-443"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85148941289"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-07273216-f588-49b0-bc52-4de53491009a"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Petrogenesis of the Late Cretaceous Sillai Patti carbonatites, NW Pakistan : Insights from C-O-Sr-Nd isotopes and titanite U[sbnd]Pb dating","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-133417","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Despite the mounting attention being paid to vegetation growth and their driving forces for water-limited ecosystems, the relative contributions of atmospheric and soil moisture dryness stress on vegetation growth are an ongoing debate. Here we comprehensively compare the impacts of high vapor pressure deficit (VPD) and low soil water content (SWC) on vegetation growth in Eurasian drylands during 1982–2014. The analysis indicates a gradual decoupling between atmospheric dryness and soil dryness over this period, as the former has expanded faster than the latter. Moreover, the VPD–SWC relation and VPD–greenness relation are both non-linear, while the SWC–greenness relation is near-linear. The loosened coupling between VPD and SWC, the non-linear correlations among VPD–SWC-greenness and the expanded area extent in which SWC acts as the dominant stress factor all provide compelling evidence that SWC is a more influential stressor than VPD on vegetation growth in Eurasian drylands. In addition, a set of 11 Earth system models projected a continuously growing constraint of SWC stress on vegetation growth towards 2100. Our results are vital to dryland ecosystems management and drought mitigation in Eurasia.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Zhang, Yu","Zhang, Yangjian","Lian, Xu","Zheng, Zhoutao","Zhao, Guang","Zhang, Tao","Xu, Minjie","Huang, Ke","Chen, Ning","Li, Ji","Piao, Shilong"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-720051"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["China","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","China University of Geosciences, Wuhan","Chinese Academy of Sciences","Columbia University","Peking University","Shenyang Agricultural University"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"enhanced dominance of soil moisture stress on vegetation growth in eurasian drylands","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwad108","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-720051","identifier":[{"id":"85164560707","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37389136","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10306363","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-4537454a-af62-4e59-9118-85486b4f6349"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-47CD011C6A7CBECF60E75A34871C4E15"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/379084550/Enhanced_dominance_of_soil_moisture_stress_on_vegetation_growth_in_Eurasian_drylands.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwad108","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"chinese academy of sciences","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Chinese Academy of Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"peking university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Peking University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"columbia university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Columbia University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"cn","key":"shenyang agricultural university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Shenyang Agricultural University","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Geosciences and Natural Resource Management, Geography"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"cn","key":